      <a href="#what-counts">What Counts as Paleo</a>
      <a href="#four-options">The Four Paleo Proteins</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#leucine">Leucine and Completeness</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Gray Areas</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Lead and Testing</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">How to Use It</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          A paleo protein powder is easy to define and harder to choose. Take out dairy, grains and legumes and you lose whey, casein, pea, soy and rice, which is most of the shelf. What remains is animal protein from beef, eggs, bones and hides, and those four sources behave very differently once they reach your muscles. Egg white is a complete, leucine-rich protein that comes closest to whey. Beef isolate and bone broth are mostly collagen-type protein with far less leucine per gram. Collagen peptides are not a complete protein at all. The labels rarely say any of this, and the prices do not follow quality. Below is what counts as paleo, how the four options compare on the numbers that matter, and the seven products we would actually shortlist.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Counts as a Paleo Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paleo is not a regulated term, so there is no seal that settles it. Most versions of the diet agree on the core exclusions: dairy, grains, legumes, refined sugar and highly processed seed oils. Meat, fish, eggs, vegetables, fruit, nuts and seeds are in. Applied to protein powder, that rule removes far more than people expect. Whey and casein are dairy. Pea, soy, lentil and fava protein are legumes. Brown rice protein is a grain. Even many blends sold as clean or natural contain at least one of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            What survives is a short list of animal proteins. Beef protein isolate is made by hydrolyzing beef tissue, usually with a large share of bone and connective tissue. Bone broth protein is broth simmered from bones and dried to a powder. Egg white protein is dried, pasteurized egg white. Collagen peptides are hydrolyzed from hides, bones or fish skin. A few products combine two of them, such as a beef and egg white blend. Hemp and pumpkin seed proteins are arguably paleo too, since seeds are allowed, but they are lower in protein per scoop and outside the scope of this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the label beyond the protein. A paleo powder can still fail the diet on its extras. Watch for maltodextrin, which is usually corn-derived, rice bran or oat fiber, soy lecithin, pea fiber, sucralose and other artificial sweeteners, and added cane sugar. Gums such as xanthan and guar are not grains or legumes, and most paleo followers accept them, but strict eaters often prefer labels without them. Sunflower lecithin is a seed product and is generally treated as fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some brands print Paleo on the front of the bag, and some print a certification mark from a paleo-focused organization. Treat those as a starting point, not the end of the check. Ingredient lists change between flavors, so an unflavored bag can be strict paleo while the chocolate version adds coconut sugar or a gum. Every product we recommend below is either single-ingredient in the version we picked or uses only paleo-compatible extras, per the label at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No milk, no grain, no bean, no artificial sweetener.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Read the full ingredient list, not the front of the bag. If the protein is beef, egg, bone broth or collagen and nothing else on the list comes from dairy, grains, legumes or a lab-made sweetener, it passes most paleo rules.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="four-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Paleo Proteins, Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            The four sources look interchangeable on a supplement-facts panel. Each lists grams of protein, and the numbers are similar: roughly 15 to 26 g a scoop. The difference is in the amino acids that make up those grams. Muscle protein synthesis depends on the essential amino acids, and leucine in particular acts as the trigger. Collagen, by contrast, is dominated by glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, which are useful for connective tissue but do little to switch on muscle building on their own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white is the outlier in the good direction. It is a complete protein with a profile close to the muscle-building end of the range, and the two egg labels in our database that print amino acids show about 2.0 to 2.3 g of leucine per 24 to 25 g of protein. That is not quite whey, but it is close enough that one scoop does the job of one whey scoop for most people. If you can eat eggs and your goal is muscle, egg white is the paleo default. Our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein guide</a> covers the category in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beef protein isolate is the most misunderstood of the four. It sounds like powdered steak, but most products are made largely from connective tissue, so the profile drifts toward collagen. Transparent Labs, for example, highlights glycine, proline and hydroxyproline on its own listing. Beef isolate is still a reasonable protein, and small trials found lean mass gains similar to whey when people took enough of it, but gram for gram it is the weaker muscle stimulus. The full comparison is in our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein powder guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bone broth protein and collagen peptides sit at the collagen end. Paleovalley publishes a typical amino acid profile for its unflavored bone broth showing about 0.58 g of leucine in 15 g of protein, under 4 percent. Collagen peptides lack tryptophan entirely, which makes them an incomplete protein by definition. Both are fine for what they are, a glycine-rich supplement for people interested in joints, skin or gut, and a top-up to daily protein. Neither should be your only post-workout shake.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
