        <div id="leucine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Leucine and Completeness: The Number That Separates Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Research on muscle protein synthesis suggests a meal or shake works best once it supplies somewhere around 2 to 3 g of leucine alongside the other essential amino acids. That is a rough target rather than a hard switch, and older adults appear to need the higher end of it. A 25 g scoop of whey clears it comfortably. A 25 g scoop of egg white lands at the bottom of the range. A scoop of beef isolate, bone broth or collagen falls well short.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap does not make the collagen-type powders useless. It means you have to dose them differently or pair them with something. The trials that compared beef protein isolate with whey gave participants roughly 46 g a day, about two scoops, and that is the sensible dose if beef isolate is your main shake. Bone broth at 15 to 20 g a scoop would need two or three scoops to reach the same protein, and still lag on leucine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Completeness matters for a different reason. A protein is complete when it supplies all nine essential amino acids in useful amounts. Collagen is missing tryptophan and is low in several others, which is why the protein-quality scoring systems used by nutrition scientists rate it near zero on its own. Your daily diet fills the gaps if you also eat meat, fish or eggs, which most paleo eaters do, so collagen is not harmful. It simply should not count as a full serving of protein when you plan a meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution about labels that print amino acids. NOW Sports adds about 1 g of branched-chain amino acids to its egg white powder, which lifts its listed leucine to roughly 1.8 g in a 15 g serving. That is real leucine and it does count, but it is not intrinsic to the egg, and you should compare like with like. For beef and bone broth powders without a printed panel, a useful rule from our beef guide applies: if glycine is several times higher than leucine, treat the product like collagen.
          </p>

