        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every animal protein is equal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Beef isolate, bone broth and collagen list similar grams of protein to egg white, but most of their protein is collagen-type and low in leucine. One scoop of egg white does roughly the job of one whey scoop. One scoop of bone broth does not come close, so plan the dose before you plan the budget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the paleo badge over the ingredient list</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flavored versions often add ingredients the unflavored bag does not have: coconut sugar, gums, natural flavors or, in mainstream brands, sucralose. Peanut butter flavors can add peanut, which is a legume. Read the full ingredient list for the exact flavor you are buying, because the paleo claim on the front may only apply to some of the range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bone broth bag with 15 g scoops and a beef isolate with 25 g scoops can carry the same price and differ by two-thirds in cost per gram. Divide price by servings, scale to 25 g of protein, and then double the figure for collagen-type powders if you rely on them as your main muscle-building shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting collagen as a complete protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen peptides lack tryptophan and are low in several essential amino acids. They still add to your daily protein total, and they are fine alongside meat, fish or eggs. They should not be the only protein in a meal you are counting on for recovery, and they should not replace a complete powder in a meal plan.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring lead testing on bone-derived powders</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bone stores lead, so bone broth and many beef isolates are worth extra scrutiny, especially if you take several scoops a day. Look for brands that publish batch results or hold a contaminant-testing certification, and rotate in egg white, which contains no bone, if you use paleo protein every day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

