        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best paleo protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people who eat eggs, an unflavored egg white powder such as Naked Egg is the best paleo protein powder, because it is complete, has about 2.3 g of leucine per 25 g of protein per its label, and costs less per gram than beef or bone broth. If you avoid eggs, Transparent Labs or Equip Unflavored beef isolate are the better choices, dosed at two scoops.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein paleo?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Whey and casein are both milk proteins, and dairy is excluded on standard paleo. Some people following a looser primal approach accept grass-fed dairy, and some tolerate a low-lactose whey isolate without trouble. If you are strict about paleo, though, whey is out, and the realistic options are egg white, beef isolate, bone broth and collagen powders.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pea protein paleo?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Peas are legumes, and legumes are one of the core paleo exclusions alongside grains and dairy. That rules out pea, soy, lentil and fava protein, and most plant blends that combine them with rice. Hemp and pumpkin seed proteins are arguably paleo because seeds are allowed, but they carry less protein per scoop than the animal options in this guide.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is bone broth protein a complete protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Bone broth protein contains all nine essential amino acids in small amounts, but it is dominated by collagen-type amino acids such as glycine and proline and is very low in leucine. Paleovalley's published profile shows about 0.58 g of leucine in 15 g of protein. Treat bone broth as a useful top-up rather than a complete muscle-building shake, and pair it with complete protein foods.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much beef protein isolate should I take per shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  If beef isolate is your main shake, take about two scoops, roughly 40 to 50 g of protein. The trials comparing beef isolate with whey used around 46 g a day, and the extra protein offsets the lower leucine. If you take one scoop, use it inside a meal that already contains eggs, meat or fish so the meal reaches a useful leucine total.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are stevia and monk fruit allowed on paleo?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most paleo eaters accept stevia and monk fruit because they are plant-derived and contain no sugar. Stricter followers avoid all intense sweeteners, and some programs based on paleo rule them out entirely. If that is you, buy the unflavored version and flavor it with cocoa, coffee, cinnamon or fruit. Sucralose and acesulfame potassium are synthetic and fail most paleo definitions.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is paleo protein powder better than whey for muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Generally not. Whey has more leucine per gram and much more research behind it. Egg white comes closest among paleo options and is a reasonable one-to-one swap. Beef isolate can produce similar results at a higher dose. Choose paleo protein because dairy does not fit your diet or does not agree with you, not because it builds muscle better.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

